## 7. Database Security and Row Level Security

Enable RLS on every table exposed through the Supabase Data API.

Create explicit policies for every required operation. Do not rely solely on frontend route guards.

### User permissions

A normal authenticated user may:

- Read and update permitted fields of their own profile.
- Create and manage their own cycle records.
- Create and manage their own daily check-ins.
- Read the available wellbeing options.
- Read active food catalog entries.
- Create and review their own food requests.
- Read and manage the items belonging to their own requests, subject to valid request states.
- Create and review their own Ring the Bell requests.
- Read permitted personal request statuses.

A normal user must not be able to read or modify another user's personal records.

### Administrator permissions

An authorized administrator may:

- Read and manage the food requests that need handling.
- Read their associated food request items.
- Update permitted food request statuses.
- Read and update administrator notifications.
- Read and update Ring the Bell requests.
- Manage appropriate food catalog records if catalog management is implemented.

An administrator must not automatically gain access to users' private cycle entries or daily mood and symptom records.

### Additional security requirements

- Every user-owned table must enforce ownership using `auth.uid()` or an equivalent secure ownership check.
- Update policies must have correct `USING` and `WITH CHECK` restrictions.
- Users must not be able to change their `user_id` to another account.
- Users must not be able to promote themselves to administrator.
- Never use frontend-provided role names as proof of authorization.
- Never disable RLS to make an application query work.
- Restrict anonymous access to private application records.
- Use server-side credentials only in trusted server environments.
- Never expose private administrative functions through unrestricted endpoints.

Review and test the final policies, not merely the frontend authorization logic.

